WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.110
A practical starting pattern is one front end with multiple inference nodes.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.200
The front end provides a stable user or application interface,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.840
while each worker hosts a model server.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.895
A router or load balancer sends requests only to workers capable of serving the requested

00:00:13.895 --> 00:00:14.607
model.

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:17.915
Replicating the same model across several nodes increases concurrent capacity and creates

00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:19.550
options for maintenance or failure,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.040
provided the routing layer removes unhealthy instances promptly.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.915
Another pattern assigns separate model roles.

00:00:24.915 --> 00:00:28.295
One node may serve a general language model, another a coding model,

00:00:28.295 --> 00:00:32.840
and a smaller CPU or GPU service may generate embeddings or rerank search results.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.845
Role separation makes resource use predictable and prevents a large model from evicting

00:00:36.845 --> 00:00:38.600
smaller services.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.260
CPU support nodes can host APIs, queues, databases, observability,

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.960
and retrieval components, preserving expensive GPU capacity for inference.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.776
Storage and management may also be separated.

00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:52.560
A storage node can hold controlled model versions and distribute them to local worker

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.120
caches.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.570
A management node can provide configuration, monitoring, secrets,

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.402
and deployment coordination.

00:00:58.402 --> 00:01:01.565
Avoid making either service an undocumented single point of failure.

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:04.930
Define what happens when shared storage, the router, name resolution,

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.000
or the management plane is unavailable.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.910
Local clusters favor privacy and predictable ownership.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.885
Cloud clusters favor rapid provisioning and elastic capacity.

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:14.510
Hybrid designs can combine them,

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:18.395
but data classification and routing policy must make the boundary explicit.

00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:20.580
Start with the fewest roles that meet the requirement.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.870
Add replication, queues,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.360
and management services in response to observed failure or capacity needs,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.400
because every architectural box becomes software that must be secured, monitored,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.560
upgraded, and recovered.
